y both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\\\\n&=1\\left[\\left(2 y\\right)\\left(\\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\\right)+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\left(x^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\\right)\\right]+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=x^{2} y^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)} + 2 y \\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=-1$ and $y=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(-1,- \\frac{\\pi}{4})&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16}\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{8} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186699145 1074 "Differentiate with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as constant. Write $f=Cuv+r$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=1,\\quad r=0\\\\\nu&=y^{2},\\quad v=\\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the power, sum, and chain rules on the active factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_y&=2 y\\\\\nv_y&=x^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\\\\\nr_y&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply both terms of the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y&=C(u_yv+uv_y)+r_y\\\\\n&=1\\left[\\left(2 y\\right)\\left(\\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\\right)+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\left(x^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\\right)\\right]+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=x^{2} y^{2} \\sec^{2}{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)} + 2 y \\tan{\\left(x^{2} y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=-1$ and $y=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and combine the exact values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y\\left(-1,- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)&=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{16}\\right)\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\pi^{2}}{8} + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186699492 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)}$, evaluate $f_y(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},-1)$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186699492 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)}$, evaluate $f_y\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},-1\\right)$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186699492 1074 "Treat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x^{2} y^{2}\\right)&=4 x^{2} y\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(x\\right)+\\left(4 x^{2} y\\right)\\\\\n&=4 x^{2} y + x \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $y=-1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\left(-1\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(- \\frac{\\pi}{2},-1)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(u_0 \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\pi^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\pi^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=- \\pi \\left(\\frac{1}{2} + \\pi\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186699492 1074 "Treat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(- \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x^{2} y^{2}\\right)&=4 x^{2} y\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(x\\right)+\\left(4 x^{2