 [17592186721924 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$, where $r>0$, and Cartesian coordinates $(2,0)$. The point $B$ has polar coordinates $\\left(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$.\n\nUse $x=R\\cos\\phi$ and $y=R\\sin\\phi$ for a point with polar coordinates $(R,\\phi)$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186721924 1074 "Since $A=(2,0)$ lies on the positive $x$-axis, its distance from the origin is $r=2$. Thus the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2r,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3})=(4,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe lesson's right triangle shows why the Cartesian coordinates of a point are its radius times the cosine and sine of its angle:\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nUsing the radius $4$ and angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ of $B$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=2,\\\\\ny_B&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2,2\\sqrt{3})$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186721924 1074 "Since $A=(2,0)$ lies on the positive $x$-axis, its distance from the origin is $r=2$. Thus the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2r,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\left(4,\\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe lesson's right triangle shows why the Cartesian coordinates of a point are its radius times the cosine and sine of its angle:\n\n![](images/20/205b3684042b0ab0261a4d56800cd89a10a7816f8022befcf6c664337f834380.png)\n\nUsing the radius $4$ and angle $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ of $B$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=4\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=2,\\\\\ny_B&=4\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=4\\cdot\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2,2\\sqrt{3})$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186721926 1080 "(-\\fra