. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$ {{field-2}}$)$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186721939 1074 "First, recover the radius of $A$ from its Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2}\\\\\n &=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2+(\\sqrt{2})^2}=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{6})$. We use $x_B=R_B\\cos\\phi_B$ and $y_B=R_B\\sin\\phi_B$ with the radius and angle of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\\\\ny_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{1}{3})$. Both coordinates are negative, as expected for an angle in the third quadrant." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186721939 1074 "First, recover the radius of $A$ from its Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr&=\\sqrt{x_A^2+y_A^2}\\\\\n &=\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2+(\\sqrt{2})^2}=\\sqrt{4}=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)$. We use $x_B=R_B\\cos\\phi_B$ and $y_B=R_B\\sin\\phi_B$ with the radius and angle of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\\\\ny_B&=\\frac{2}{3}\\sin\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\frac{2}{3}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$. Both coordinates are negative, as expected for an angle in the third quadrant." 13194139545761 17592