}{x}$.\n\n![](images/7f/7fac7995dafe59d6258005e53830f1b7e66c46688df13ca15abaf8eb56f9aaca.png)\n\nStretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to obtain $y=-\\frac{3}{2x}$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f156962b95a68b9f84348c80589bf684bd66b109510542d7abf5a0594a79da9.png)\n\nTranslate this curve $1$ unit down to obtain $y=-\\frac{3}{2x}-1$. The vertical asymptote is $x=0$ and the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1$.\n\nWhen $x=-1$, $y=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}$; when $x=1$, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}-1=-\\frac{5}{2}$. Thus the graph passes through $(-1,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(1,-\\frac{5}{2})$:\n\n![](images/94/944c065f6026a0da8eb67589fdd0c9aa3ec80d902a9fd3b86a95c58c9a952488.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186736483 1074 "Start with the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$.\n\n![](images/7f/7fac7995dafe59d6258005e53830f1b7e66c46688df13ca15abaf8eb56f9aaca.png)\n\nStretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to obtain $y=-\\frac{3}{2x}$.\n\n![](images/6f/6f156962b95a68b9f84348c80589bf684bd66b109510542d7abf5a0594a79da9.png)\n\nTranslate this curve $1$ unit down to obtain $y=-\\frac{3}{2x}-1$. The vertical asymptote is $x=0$ and the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1$.\n\nWhen $x=-1$, $y=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}$; when $x=1$, $y=-\\frac{3}{2}-1=-\\frac{5}{2}$. Thus the graph passes through $\\left(-1,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(1,-\\frac{5}{2}\\right)$:\n\n![](images/94/944c065f6026a0da8eb67589fdd0c9aa3ec80d902a9fd3b86a95c58c9a952488.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186736487 1074 "Start with the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$.\n\n![](images/7f/7fac7995dafe59d6258005e53830f1b7e66c46688df13ca15abaf8eb56f9aaca.png)\n\nTranslate it $1$ unit to the left to obtain $y=-\\frac{1}{x+1}$.\n\n![](images/d0/d0bca969cf0559093a171acd247c9daf5e7924270c1348645b71a77f6c73467e.png)\n\nThen stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to obtain $y=-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}$.\n\nThe vertical asymptote of the required graph is $x=-1$ and its horizontal asymptote is $y=0$. When $x=-2$, $y=-\\frac{3}{2(-1)}=\\frac{3}{2}$; when $x=0$, $y=-\\frac{3}{2(1)}=-\\frac{3}{2}$. The final graph passes through $(-2,\\frac{3}{2})$ and $(0,-\\frac{3}{2})$:\n\n![](images/b8/b879d2ad5e5693674219a2028b4be2b5635fc7bec3ff85ad9258b993189892f3.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186736487 1074 "Start with the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$.\n\n![](images/7f/7fac7995dafe59d6258005e53830f1b7e66c46688df13ca15abaf8eb56f9aaca.png)\n\nTranslate it $1$ unit to the left to obtain $y=-\\frac{1}{x+1}$.\n\n![](images/d0/d0bca969cf0559093a171acd247c9daf5e7924270c1348645b71a77f6c73467e.png)\n\nThen stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{3}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to obtain $y=-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}$.\n\nThe vertical asymptote of the required graph is $x=-1$ and its horizontal asymptote is $y=0$. When $x=-2$, $y=-\\frac{3}{2(-1)}=\\frac{3}{2}$; when $x=0$, $y=-\\frac{3}{2(1)}=-\\frac{3}{2}$. The final graph passes through $\\left(-2,\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(0,-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$:\n\n![](images/b8/b879d2ad5e5693674219a2028b4be2b5635fc7bec3ff85ad9258b993189892f3.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186736671 1074 "To plot $y=\\frac{4}{3x},$ start with the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/96/9645654d76e1a0b917e27c92f1a5273a333f2e5215fd79c4ec91c7ecdbfa2070.png)\n\nMultiply every $y$-coordinate by $\\frac{4}{3}$, keeping the $x$-coordinate fixed. This is a stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{4}{3}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, $(1,1)$ maps to $(1,\\frac{4}{3})$, and $(-1,-1)$ maps to $(-1,-\\frac{4}{3})$. The asymptotes remain $x=0$ and $y=0$, and the branches lie in quadrants I and III. The required graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/51/51cd2d066d85dea560b3ddc351c458afd7eea7423b5fb4c98b51bd75ac6f9ab6.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186736671 1074 "To plot $y=\\frac{4}{3x},$ start with the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/96/9645654d76e1a0b917e27c92f1a5273a333f2e5215fd79c4ec91c7ecdbfa2070.png)\n\nMultiply every $y$-coordinate by $\\frac{4}{3}$, keeping the $x$-coordinate fixed. This is a stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{4}{3}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, $(1,1)$ maps to $\\left(1,\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$, and $(-1,-1)$ maps to $\\left(-1,-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$. The asymptotes remain $x=0$ and $y=0$, and the branches lie in quadrants I and III. The required graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/51/51cd2d066d85dea560b3ddc351c458afd7eea7423b5fb4c98b51bd75ac6f9ab6.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186736678 1074 "To plot $y=\\frac{7}{4x},$ start with the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/92/9299a724fd44ac72d0c2fffe83dec707213900e942f25788f29794d46cc1fd81.png)\n\nMultiply every $y$-coordinate by $\\frac{7}{4}$, keeping the $x$-coordinate fixed. This is a stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{7}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nAt $x=2$, the original curve has $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, so the new height is $\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{8}$. Thus $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ maps to $(2,\\frac{7}{8})$, and $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2})$ maps to $(-2,-\\frac{7}{8})$. The asymptotes remain $x=0$ and $y=0$, and the branches lie in quadrants I and III. The required graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/be/be92f2eaf2351289259a4d810549f260453e138a2b40ee213b9f7f6ff285003a.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186736678 1074 "To plot $y=\\frac{7}{4x},$ start with the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/92/9299a724fd44ac72d0c2fffe83dec707213900e942f25788f29794d46cc1fd81.png)\n\nMultiply every $y$-coordinate by $\\frac{7}{4}$, keeping the $x$-coordinate fixed. This is a stretch by a scale factor of $\\frac{7}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nAt $x=2$, the original curve has $y=\\frac{1}{2}$, so the new height is $\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{8}$. Thus $\\left(2,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ maps to $\\left(2,\\frac{7}{8}\\right)$, and $\\left(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ maps to $\\left(-2,-\\frac{7}{8}\\right)$. The asymptotes remain $x=0$ and $y=0$, and the branches lie in quadrants I and III. The required graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/be/be92f2eaf2351289259a4d810549f260453e138a2b40ee213b9f7f6ff285003a.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186736721 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{4}{3x},$ start with the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/6a/6ae557eca7c4c743e6eddf9231b85ffdd87f02869ed8dd153023bc5b52b96f9c.png)\n\nStretch this curve by a scale factor of $\\frac{4}{3}$ parallel to the $y$-axis. The coordinate axes remain the asymptotes, and the branches remain in the second and fourth quadrants.\n\nThe points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ map to $(-1,\\frac{4}{3})$ and $(1,-\\frac{4}{3})$. The required graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd2be2dcf36d97a11ff4750a04636b363760123ce0300eda67d19402e1df1a65.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186736721 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{4}{3x},$ start with the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/6a/6ae557eca7c4c743e6eddf9231b85ffdd87f02869ed8dd153023bc5b52b96f9c.png)\n\nStretch this curve by a scale factor of $\\frac{4}{3}$ parallel to the $y$-axis. The coordinate axes remain the asymptotes, and the branches remain in the second and fourth quadrants.\n\nThe points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ map to $\\left(-1,\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$ and $\\left(1,-\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$. The required graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd2be2dcf36d97a11ff4750a04636b363760123ce0300eda67d19402e1df1a65.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186736729 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{5}{6x},$ start with the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d0/d0eb68d7abdb8ed8e145e4ab0cc25feb20c96f8c56c4634aaac232f0e810a8fa.png)\n\nStretch this curve by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{6}$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This is a vertical contraction because the scale factor is less than $1$. The coordinate axes remain the asymptotes, and the branches remain in the second and fourth quadrants.\n\nOn $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$, the points at $x=-2$ and $x=2$ are $(-2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(2,-\\frac{1}{2})$. Multiplying their ordinates by $\\frac{5}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{12},\\qquad \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{12}.\n$$\n\nThus the transformed curve passes through $(-2,\\frac{5}{12})$ and $(2,-\\frac{5}{12})$. The required graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95405b4a37f2492b428056aa6c81d12b2acd94a6814db6302c1f72149d91c2d7.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186736729 1074 "To plot $y=-\\frac{5}{6x},$ start with the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/d0/d0eb68d7abdb8ed8e145e4ab0cc25feb20c96f8c56c4634aaac232f0e810a8fa.png)\n\nStretch this curve by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{6}$ parallel to the $y$-axis. This is a vertical contraction because the scale factor is less than $1$. The coordinate axes remain the asymptotes, and the branches remain in the second and fourth quadrants.\n\nOn $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$, the points at $x=-2$ and $x=2$ are $\\left(-2,\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ and $\\left(2,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$. Multiplying their ordinates by $\\frac{5}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{12},\\qquad \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{5}{12}.\n$$\n\nThus the transformed curve passes through $\\left(-2,\\frac{5}{12}\\right)$ and $\\left(2,-\\frac{5}{12}\\right)$. The required graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/95/95405b4a37f2492b428056aa6c81d12b2acd94a6814db6302c1f72149d91c2d7.png)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186736936 1074 "Start with the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}$.\n\n![](images/30/30a7bc0c953dce9fc0887f79c6f20979298be5cd27bb17477d944364e8f7e20b.png)\n\nStretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to obtain $y=\\frac{1}{2x}$.\n\n![](images/bd/bd8e8f178ec4e7d8f8d649d999aab28ef4a93fba7f357603be3c8c56db00d2b6.png)\n\nTranslate this curve $3$ units down to obtain $y=\\frac{1}{2x}-3$.\n\n![](images/ca/ca0b5519c2077f622346623e787b488521ec07e96b4272cc13bbe9bf51c8aa4c.png)\n\nThe vert