he extremum type. Thus the local maximum point is $\\left(\\frac{1}{3},-2\\right)$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186741004 1080 "(\\frac{1}{3},0)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186741004 1080 "\\left(\\frac{1}{3},0\\right)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186741007 1080 "(-\\frac{5}{3},-1)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186741007 1080 "\\left(-\\frac{5}{3},-1\\right)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186741010 1080 "(-\\frac{2}{3},-5)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186741010 1080 "\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},-5\\right)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186741018 1080 "(-\\frac{2}{3},-1)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186741018 1080 "\\left(-\\frac{2}{3},-1\\right)" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186741497 1071 "The rate $O$ of the orders a car factory receives can be modeled as $O(t)=130t{e}^{-0.2t}$ cars per day. The rate at which cars are completed can be modeled as $C(t)=30+15\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{10}t)$ cars per day. In both cases, $t$ is the time, in days, since the launch of the new model. \n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction $20$ days after the model's launch? Assume that an ordered car goes into construction as soon as the factory receives its order. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186741497 1071 "The rate $O$ of the orders a car factory receives can be modeled as $O(t)=130t{e}^{-0.2t}$ cars per day. The rate at which cars are completed can be modeled as $C(t)=30+15\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{10}t\\right)$ cars per day. In both cases, $t$ is the time, in days, since the launch of the new model. \n\nWhat is the rate of change in the number of cars under construction $20$ days after the model's launch? Assume that an ordered car goes into construction as soon as the factory receives its order. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186741497 1074 "The rate of change of the number of cars under construction at time $t$ is equal to the order rate minus the completion rate, that is,\n\n$$\nO(t)-C(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=20,$ the rate of change in the number of cars under construction after $20$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(t)-C(t) & =130(20){e}^{-0.2(20)}-(30+15\\sin(\\frac{2\\pi }{10}(20))) \\\\  & =130(20){e}^{-4}-30-15\\sin(4 \\pi) \\\\  & =130(20){e}^{-4}-30 \\\\  & ≈18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of cars in construction after $20$ days is $18$ cars per day. The quantity is increasing." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186741497 1074 "The rate of change of the number of cars under construction at time $t$ is equal to the order rate minus the completion rate, that is,\n\n$$\nO(t)-C(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $t=20,$ the rate of change in the number of cars under construction after $20$ days is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}O(t)-C(t) & =130(20){e}^{-0.2(20)}-\\left(30+15\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{10}(20)\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =130(20){e}^{-4}-30-15\\sin(4 \\pi) \\\\  & =130(20){e}^{-4}-30 \\\\  & ≈18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change in the number of cars in construction after $20$ days is $18$ cars per day. The quantity is increasing." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186741879 1074 "The slope is the value of $y'(x)$ at $x=e^2$. Let $u(x)=x+\\ln x$ and $v(x)=x-\\ln x$. Their derivatives are\n\n$$\nu'(x)=1+\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad v'(x)=1-\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule and keeping the subtraction around the entire second product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny'(x)&=\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{(1+\\frac{1}{x})(x-\\ln x)-(x+\\ln x)(1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(x-\\ln x)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x-\\ln x+1-\\frac{\\ln x}{x}-(x+\\ln x-1-\\frac{\\ln x}{x})}{(x-\\ln x)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2-2\\ln x}{(x-\\ln x)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=e^2$, we have $\\ln(e^2)=2$ and the original denominator is $e^2-2\\ne 0$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny'(e^2)&=\\frac{2-2(2)}{(e^2-2)^2}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{(e^2-2)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $-\\frac{2}{(e^2-2)^2}$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186741879 1074 "The slope is the value of $y'(x)$ at $x=e^2$. Let $u(x)=x+\\ln x$ and $v(x)=x-\\ln x$. Their derivatives are\n\n$$\nu'(x)=1+\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad v'(x)=1-\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule and keeping the subtraction around the entire second product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny'(x)&=\\frac{u'(x)v(x)-u(x)v'(x)}{[v(x)]^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{\\left(1+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)(x-\\ln x)-(x+\\ln x)\\left(1-\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{(x-\\ln x)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{x-\\ln x+1-\\frac{\\ln x}{x}-\\left(x+\\ln x-1-\\frac{\\ln x}{x}\\right)}{(x-\\ln x)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{2-2\\ln x}{(x-\\ln x)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=e^2$, we have $\\ln(e^2)=2$ and the original denominator is $e^2-2\\ne 0$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny'(e^2)&=\\frac{2-2(2)}{(e^2-2)^2}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{(e^2-2)^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $-\\frac{2}{(e^2-2)^2}$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186742766 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\cos x}{2}+\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=(\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sin x))+(\\frac{1}{2}(2)x) \\\\ &=-\\frac{\\sin x}{2}+x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186742766 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\cos x}{2}+\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(-\\sin x)\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(2)x\\right) \\\\ &=-\\frac{\\sin x}{2}+x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186742770 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\cos x+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=(1(-\\sin x))+(\\frac{1}{3}(3)x^{2}) \\\\ &=-\\sin x+x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186742770 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\cos x+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=(1(-\\sin x))+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(3)x^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=-\\sin x+x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186742780 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{2}-2\\cos x+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}+\\frac{1}{x^{3}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{2}-2\\cos x+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}+x^{-3}\\right) \\\\ &=(\\frac{1}{2}(\\cos x))+(-2(-\\sin x)) \\\\ &\\quad{}+(\\frac{1}{3}(3)x^{2})+(1(-3)x^{-4}) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\cos x}{2}+2\\sin x+x^{2}-\\frac{3}{x^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function and derivative have domain $x\\ne0$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186742780 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{2}-2\\cos x+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}+\\frac{1}{x^{3}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\sin x}{2}-2\\cos x+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}+x^{-3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(\\cos x)\\right)+(-2(-\\sin x)) \\\\ &\\quad{}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}(3)x^{2}\\right)+(1(-3)x^{-4}) \\\\ &=\\frac{\\cos x}{2}+2\\sin x+x^{2}-\\frac{3}{x^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function and derivative have domain $x\\ne0$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186742782 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(-\\frac{\\sin x}{2}+2\\cos x+\\frac{x^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(-\\frac{\\sin x}{2}+2\\cos x+\\frac{x^{2}}{2}-x^{-2}\\right) \\\\ &=(\\frac{-1}{2}(\\cos x))+(2(-\\sin x)) \\\\ &\\quad{}+(\\frac{1}{2}(2)x)+(-1(-2)x^{-3}) \\\\ &=-\\frac{\\cos x}{2}-2\\sin x+x+\\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function and derivative have domain $x\\ne0$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186742782 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(-\\frac{\\sin x}{2}+2\\cos x+\\frac{x^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(-\\frac{\\sin x}{2}+2\\cos x+\\frac{x^{2}}{2}-x^{-2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{-1}{2}(\\cos x)\\right)+(2(-\\sin x)) \\\\ &\\quad{}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}(2)x\\right)+(-1(-2)x^{-3}) \\\\ &=-\\frac{\\cos x}{2}-2\\sin x+x+\\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function and derivative have domain $x\\ne0$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 true] [17592186742786 1074 "Use $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin x)=\\cos x$ and $\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x)=-\\sin x$, together with the sum, constant multiple, and power rules.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\cos x}{3}+2\\sin x+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{\\cos x}{3}+2\\sin x+\\frac{x^{3}}{3}+x^{-1}\\right) \\\\ &=(\\frac{1}{3}(-\\sin x))+(2(\\cos x)) \\\\ &\\quad{}+(\\frac{1}{3}(3)x^{2})+(1(-1)x^{-2}) \\\\ &=-\\frac{\\sin x}{3}+2\\cos x+x^{2}-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function and derivative have domain $x\\ne0$." 13194139545761 17592186576411 false] [17592186742786 1074 